<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yahuarcocha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lake Yahuarcocha, Lake of Blood</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Yahuarcocha is a lake near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibarra">Ibarra</a> in Imbabura province, northern Ecuador. Its name means 'lake of blood' in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a>: tradition holds that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> massacred thousands of Caranqui and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayambi">Cayambi</a> warriors there in the late 15th century, staining its waters red. The victory completed the Inca conquest of northern Ecuador.</p></div>
